---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Where this came from"
  description="A non-technical operator. Real software, shipped with AI agents. The hard moves, written down. The verification discipline behind every claim on this site."
  pageType="article"
>
  <div class="container">
    <p class="kicker">About</p>
    <h1 class="h-display chapter">Where this came from.</h1>

    <div class="origin-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="body-copy">
          I am not an engineer. I was a contractor, a real estate manager, a salesman. Before I
          started operating AI agents, the most code I had written in a decade was an HTML page for
          a friend.
        </p>
        <p class="body-copy">
          A while back I was brought in to rebuild a software ecosystem for a company. No
          engineering team behind me &mdash; just me and an AI agent. The agent was the engineer. I
          was the operator: <em>set the bar, refuse the menu, verify the work.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="body-copy">
          A thousand sessions in, I had a notebook of patterns. The agent failed the same way over
          and over. <em>"Please remember"</em> never worked. <em
            >"Build a rule the agent can't ignore"</em
          > almost always did. <em>"What do you want me to do?"</em> stalled the work. <em
            >"You decide and tell me why"</em
          > moved it.
        </p>
        <p class="body-copy">
          This manual is the patterns. Every entry is a thing I saw happen, said the wrong words to,
          and then learned the right words for. Every count on the receipts page is a real count,
          from a real corpus of working sessions, redacted of anything that would identify the work.
        </p>
        <p class="body-copy">
          It is written for the next person who finds themselves doing what I did: <em
            >building real software with AI agents, without a technical background, without a team
            to fall back on.</em
          >
        </p>

        <hr class="softline" />

        <h2 class="kicker" style="margin-bottom:14px">The verification discipline</h2>
        <p class="body-copy">
          Every number on this site is a real number from real sessions. If a section's claim can't
          be traced back to a count, the section stays empty. Guessing is the failure mode this
          manual is trying to break &mdash; the manual itself doesn't guess.
        </p>
        <p class="body-copy">
          The pipeline runs on the first of each month and re-publishes the deltas. If a category
          gets more common, the count goes up. If a mechanical rule fixes a recurring pattern, the
          count goes down. The site moves with the corpus.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="receipts" aria-label="Corpus shape">
        <header class="head">
          <span class="title">Corpus shape</span>
          <span class="source">2026-05-14</span>
        </header>
        <div class="stat">
          <div class="label">Working sessions distilled</div><div class="val">1,073</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stat"><div class="label">Projects covered</div><div class="val">46</div></div>
        <div class="stat">
          <div class="label">Behaviors catalogued</div><div class="val">145</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stat"><div class="label">Principles named</div><div class="val">80</div></div>
        <div class="stat">
          <div class="label">Total user messages</div><div class="val">53,178</div>
        </div>
        <p class="note">
          Counts are aggregate. No client name, project name, or session identifier is exposed on
          this site. The underlying transcripts are private working artifacts.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>

    <div class="rev-strip">
      <span>About &middot; The story</span>
      <span>Verification discipline &middot; Living document</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
